s/TheErk/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/TheErk/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/TheErk/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/TheErk/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/TheErk/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/TheErk/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/TheErk/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/TheErk/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/TheErk/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2020-01-03T15:48:33Z","updated_at":"2020-01-03T15:49:28Z","body":"I was hit by something similar yesterday using poetry 1.0.0.\r\nI have a package whose directory structure is:\r\nmodule_name\r\n├── src\r\n│   └── module_name\r\n└── tests\r\n    └── data\r\n\r\nmy pyproject.toml has package description:\r\n```toml\r\npackages = [\r\n    { include = \"module_name\", from = \"src\" },\r\n#    { include = \"tests\", format = \"sdist\" },\r\n]\r\n```\r\n\r\nI wanted to include tests dir in the source distribution (only) but If I do that `poetry install` will fail telling me that there is no \"tests\" package in \"module_name\".\r\n\r\nAs explaine in #1109 this should work, unless I missed something?\r\n\r\nNB: the packaging with `poetry build`works as expected but `poetry  install` fails.","author_association":"NONE","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/python-poetry/poetry/issues/comments/570610706/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"event":"commented","actor":{"login":"TheErk","id":356288,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM1NjI4OA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/356288?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/TheErk","html_url":"https://github.com/TheErk","followers_url":"https://api.github.c